+(duration-opts)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. A value is either a whole
+number of seconds, such as `120`, or one or more `<integer><unit>` groups written without spaces, such as `2d`,
+`1h30m`, `90m` or `1d2h3m4s`. The units are `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m` (minutes) and `s` (seconds). Each unit
+may be used at most once, the units must appear in that order, and they are case-insensitive. Negative values, decimals
+and other units are rejected. A {class}`~datetime.timedelta` passed as a default or from Python is used unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting for {timeout}.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=["--timeout=1h30m"])
+    invoke(wait, args=["--timeout=90"])
+    invoke(wait, args=["--timeout=30m1h"])
+    println()
+    invoke(wait, args=["--help"])
+```
+
